course: dictionary
lesson: 15921
title: Unploughed: fields, roads and the prefix un-
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word unploughed is made of three parts. Un- means not, plough means to dig up and turn over the soil, and -ed shows the state of being. So unploughed means not dug up and turned over with a plough.
explain: Unploughed land is still grass, stubble (the short stalks left after a crop is cut), or wild ground. «The unploughed meadow was full of wild flowers.» The flowers grow there because nobody has turned the soil over.
explain: The word can also describe roads and paths. «After the storm, the unploughed road was deep in snow.» Here it means the snow has not yet been cleared by a snowplough.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If the field was not turned over, the sentence still makes sense. Remember the clue un-: it often means not, so look for that first.
fact: Unploughed means not dug up and turned over with a plough.
fact: The prefix un- means not, so unploughed means not ploughed.
fact: Unploughed is an adjective, a word that describes a noun. In US spelling it is «unplowed».
fact: Unploughed land can be grass, stubble or wild ground, and it can help keep soil and wildlife safe.
fact: In snowy places such as Canada and the northern USA, an «unploughed» road is one the snowplough has not yet cleared.
exercise: «The unploughed meadow was full of wild flowers.» What does unploughed mean here? | Covered in water after the rain | *Not dug up and turned over with a plough | Painted bright green | Freshly cut and piled high
exercise: Mia's bus cannot get down the «unploughed» road in Canada. What does unploughed road mean? | A road with no cars on it | A road made of soft mud | A road that has just been painted | *A road the snowplough has not yet cleared
exercise: In unploughed, the prefix un- means which of these? | under | *not | very | again
test: «The farmer left the far field unploughed, so the grass and wild flowers could grow.» Why was the field unploughed? | The field was covered in deep snow | The field was too small to use | The farmer had ploughed it twice that week | *The farmer did not dig it up and turn it over
test: In history, settlers wanted to turn the unploughed prairie into farms. What is unploughed prairie? | *Wild grassland that has never been turned over with a plough | Farmland that is already full of crops | Land at the bottom of the sea | A town with many houses and shops
exam: Which sentence uses unploughed correctly? | Mum unploughed the washing up quickly after tea. | The unploughed dog barked at the cat. | *The unploughed meadow was full of grass and wild flowers. | The unploughed cake was eaten up at the party.
